                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/equality+and+equivalence+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#using_the_interval_type"} Using the interval type
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#using_dependent_sum_types_of_identity_types"} Using dependent sum types of identity types
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#decidability"} Decidability
              %li
                %a{:href => "#justification"} Justification
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Extensional type theory
            denotes the flavor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            satisfy the
            = succeed "," do
              %em reflection rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typally equal
            then they are also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmentally equal
          %p
            In particular, this implies that all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            \/ of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-level+1"} h-level 1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-sets"} h-sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+type+theory"} set-level type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+foundations"} set-level foundations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UIP"} UIP
            as an axiom.
          %p
            %strong Note:
            %em
              For a while, the nLab incorrectly used “extensional type theory” to refer to what we now call
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+type+theory"} set-level type theory
          %p Extensional type theory is poorly behaved metatheoretically, and very difficult to implement in a proof assistant. However, it is sometimes more convenient to work with informally, and there are conservativity theorems relating it to other set-level type theories that are better-behaved.
          %p
            Type theory which is not extensional is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The word “extensional” in type theory (even when applied to identity types) sometimes refers instead to the axiom of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
            %p
              In particular,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional
              in that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
              are crucially
              %em not
              demanded to be
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
              is often assumed (in terms of these intensional identity types, of course) — in particular, it follows from the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensionality+principle"} extensionality principle
              for the universe (Hofmann and Streicher originally introduced it under the name “universe extensionality”), but it is inconsistent with “extensional type theory” in the sense considered here.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The origin of the names “extensional” and “intensional” is somewhat confusing. In fact they refer to the behavior of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
              is always an “extensional” notion of equality (although it can be more or less extensional, depending on whether further extensionality principles like
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
              hold). Thus, if the definitional equality
              %em coincides
              with the identity type, as it does under the reflection rule, the former is also extensional, and so we call the type theory “extensional” — while if the two equalities do
              %em not
              coincide, then the definitional equality has room to be more intensional than the identity type, and so we call the type theory “intensional”.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The Martin-Lof definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
            family makes them intensional. To make the type theory extensional, we add a rule that any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabitant"} inhabitant
            of an identity type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:Id_A(x,y)
            induces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{p:Id_A(x,y)}{x\equiv y}
          %p
            At first, this may appear to be only a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"}> skeletality
            ” assumption, since it does not assert explicitly that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"} reflexivity
            rather than a nontrivial
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} loop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi refl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x:A),(y:A),(p:Id_A(x,y)) \;\vdash\; Id_{Id_A(x,y)}(p,refl(x)).
          %p
            This is well-typed because the reflection rule applied to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            yields a judgmental equality
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\equiv y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi refl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} refl(x):Id_A(x,y)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution"} substituting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi refl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} refl(x)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            yields the type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Id
                          %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi refl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi refl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_{Id_A(x,x)}(refl(x),refl(x))
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi refl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi refl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} refl(refl(x))
          %p
            Thus, by induction on identity, we have a term in the above type, witnessing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal equality
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi refl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} refl(x)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi refl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\equiv refl(x)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p On the other hand, if in addition to the equality reflection rule we postulate that all equality proofs are definitionally equal to reflexivity, then we can derive the induction rule for identity types. Extensional type theory is often presented in this form.
          %p
            A different, also equivalent, way of presenting extensional type theory is with a definitional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta-conversion"} eta-conversion
            rule for the identity types; see
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "identity+type#EtaConversion"} here
          %h3#using_the_interval_type Using the interval type
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+type"} interval type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{I}
            with judgmental computation rules for the point constructors, it suffices for the two point constructors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝕀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0:\mathbb{I}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝕀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1:\mathbb{I}
            to be definitionally equal:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≡
                      %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝕀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}}{\Gamma \vdash 0 \equiv 1:\mathbb{I}}
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              By the recursion principle of the interval type, for elements
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:\mathrm{Id}_A(x, y)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} rec
                      %mi 𝕀
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝕀
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{rec}_\mathbb{I}^A(x, y, p):\mathbb{I} \to A
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} rec
                      %mi 𝕀
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{rec}_\mathbb{I}^A(x, y, p, 0) \equiv x
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} rec
                        %mi 𝕀
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{rec}_\mathbb{I}^A(x, y, p, 1) \equiv y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≡
                      %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝕀
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \equiv 1:\mathbb{I}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence+rules"} congruence rules
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≡
                    %msubsup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} rec
                      %mi 𝕀
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≡
                    %msubsup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} rec
                      %mi 𝕀
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi y
